---
import MainLayout from '../../layouts/MainLayout.astro';

const clone = `git clone <repo-url> Salesdam-v3
cd Salesdam-v3`;

const backendEnv = `# backend/.env
NODE_ENV=development
PORT=5000
MONGO_URI=mongodb://localhost:27017/salesdam
CORS_ORIGINS=http://localhost:5173

# Email (optional in development — messages are logged instead)
SMTP_HOST=
SMTP_PORT=587
SMTP_USER=
SMTP_PASS=
SMTP_SECURE=false`;

const frontendEnv = `# frontend/.env
VITE_API_URL=http://localhost:5000/api/v1

# Feature flags — 'true' enables, anything else disables
VITE_FEATURE_DASHBOARDS=true
VITE_FEATURE_AUTOMATION=true
VITE_FEATURE_EMAIL_TEMPLATES=true
VITE_FEATURE_EMAIL_SENDERS=true
VITE_FEATURE_PDF_TEMPLATES=true
VITE_FEATURE_INTEGRATIONS=true`;

const docsEnv = `# docs/.env
# Set to require an access key to read the docs. Leave unset for open docs.
# NOT prefixed PUBLIC_ on purpose: PUBLIC_ vars are inlined into the client
# bundle, which would publish the key to anyone with devtools.
DOCS_ACCESS_KEY=

# Where the explorer's "Try it" sends requests
PUBLIC_API_URL=http://localhost:5000/api/v1
PUBLIC_APP_URL=http://localhost:5173`;

const install = `# Backend
cd backend
npm install
npm run db:up        # starts MongoDB in Docker, or run your own
npm run seed         # standard objects, fields and a first tenant
npm run dev          # http://localhost:5000

# Frontend, in a second terminal
cd frontend
npm install
npm run dev          # http://localhost:5173

# Docs, in a third
cd docs
npm install
npm run dev`;
---

<MainLayout
  title="Installation"
  description="Prerequisites, environment variables and running the Salesdam stack locally."
>
  <h1>Installation</h1>
  <p class="lead">
    Four independent Node applications share one repository: the API, the app, this
    documentation site, and the marketing site. Each has its own
    <code>package.json</code> and its own <code>.env</code>.
  </p>

  <h2>Prerequisites</h2>
  <div class="table-scroll">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Requirement</th><th>Version</th><th>Notes</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Node.js</td><td>20 or newer</td><td>The backend is ESM throughout.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>MongoDB</td><td>6 or newer</td><td>A replica set is required — the record write paths use transactions.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Docker</td><td>Any recent</td><td>Optional. <code>npm run db:up</code> starts a suitable MongoDB for you.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <div class="warning">
    <strong>MongoDB must support transactions.</strong> Record creates and updates
    run in a session so that validation, auto-numbers, rollups and formulas either
    all land or none do. A standalone <code>mongod</code> without a replica set will
    fail on write. <code>npm run db:up</code> provisions one correctly.
  </div>

  <h2>Clone</h2>
  <pre><code>{clone}</code></pre>

  <h2>Environment</h2>
  <h3>Backend</h3>
  <pre><code>{backendEnv}</code></pre>
  <p>
    Sessions need no secret: the <code>crm.sid</code> cookie holds an opaque session id
    that is looked up in MongoDB, so signing out (or deleting the session record) ends it.
    <code>CORS_ORIGINS</code> must include the frontend's origin.
  </p>

  <h3>Frontend</h3>
  <pre><code>{frontendEnv}</code></pre>
  <p>
    Vite only exposes variables prefixed <code>VITE_</code> to the browser. Feature
    flags are read in <code>src/config/features.js</code> and compared against the
    string <code>'true'</code>, so any other value disables the feature.
  </p>

  <h3>Docs</h3>
  <pre><code>{docsEnv}</code></pre>

  <h2>Install and run</h2>
  <pre><code>{install}</code></pre>

  <div class="note">
    <strong>Seed before first login.</strong> <code>npm run seed</code> creates the
    standard objects, their fields and an initial tenant. Without it the app loads
    with no objects and the sidebar is empty.
  </div>

  <h2>Ports</h2>
  <div class="table-scroll">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Service</th><th>Port</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>API</td><td><code>5000</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td>App</td><td><code>5173</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Docs</td><td><code>4321</code> (Astro's default; the marketing site also claims it — run one at a time or pass <code>--port</code>)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>MongoDB</td><td><code>27017</code></td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2>Verifying</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><code>GET http://localhost:5000/health</code> should return a success envelope.</li>
    <li>The app should load its login page at <code>http://localhost:5173</code>.</li>
    <li>Sign in, then check the sidebar lists the seeded objects.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Common problems</h2>
  <div class="table-scroll">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Symptom</th><th>Cause</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>Writes fail with a transaction error</td>
          <td>MongoDB is not running as a replica set. Use <code>npm run db:up</code>.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>App loads but every request 401s</td>
          <td><code>VITE_API_URL</code> points somewhere else, or the API isn't running.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Login succeeds, next request 401s</td>
          <td>Cookies are being dropped — check the API's CORS origin and that credentials are allowed.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Sidebar is empty after login</td>
          <td>The seed hasn't run.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Docs "Try it" fails with a network error</td>
          <td>The docs origin isn't on the API's CORS allow-list, or <code>PUBLIC_API_URL</code> is wrong.</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
</MainLayout>
